  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "R1 and R2 already apply atomistic uniaxial tension to closely related FCC high-entropy alloys at temperatures and strain rates overlapping the later study; R1 additionally identifies partial-dislocation nucleation and FCC-to-HCP-related deformation. R20 directly establishes temperature-dependent intrinsic stacking-fault energetics in the closely related Cantor alloy, while R6, R7, R9, R13, R15, and R16 provide supporting links among temperature, dislocation mobility or density, and strength. Together these records make a systematic MD study varying crystallographic orientation, temperature, and strain rate readily proposable, with Shockley partials and stacking faults as the expected mediators. The specific emphasis on vacancy-cluster nucleation, negligible yield-rate sensitivity, the <-111> strength maximum, and absence of twinning or bulk transformation is not directly supplied, but these are refinements rather than a substantial new intervention concept."
